  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nA ridge of high pressure continues to reside over the Great \nLakes in between the Nor'easter off the east coast and the broad\ntroughing over the Pacific Northwest and Mountain West regions.\nThis ridge is expected to remain in place through at least \nMonday which will keep seasonable temperatures (highs in the \nupper 60s to lower 70s and lows in the upper 40s and 50s) and \nprimarily dry conditions around locally. However, there has been\na band of some light showers and sprinkles in eastern IA \nassociated with a shortwave disturbance rounding the base of the\naforementioned western trough. While these showers/sprinkles \nwill likely drift eastward tonight, the persistent east-\nnortheast winds should keep drier air in place and prevent much \nof these showers/sprinkles from reaching the ground. That said, \nhave opted to maintain a 10-20% chance for an isolated sprinkle \nnear and west of I-39 late tonight into early Sunday morning \njust in case. Additionally, there is a chance for some patchy \nfog to develop around daybreak Sunday morning, mainly in \nnorthwest IN and adjacent areas of northeast IL. Given that \ncirrus clouds from the aforementioned showers in IA will spread \neast tonight, it is unclear how widespread and dense the fog \nwill be as the clouds should help to ease some of the \nradiational cooling. For now have introduced some patchy fog to \nthe forecast in northwest IN and portions of northeast IL where \ncloud cover is forecast to be at a minimum. \n\nBy Tuesday the broad trough over the Pacific Northwest and \nMountain West is forecast to be ejecting into the upper Midwest.\nAs it does so another trough/closed upper low is forecast to be\ntraversing across southern California and the southwest CONUS. \nLatest ensemble and deterministic guidance favors that these two\nfeatures will remain separate from one another which means that\nthe Pacific Northwest trough should arrive first and bring with\nit some rain showers for the Tuesday night into Wednesday night\ntimeframe. The southwestern trough/low is then expected to lag \nand arrive sometime towards the Thursday into Friday timeframe \nwith additional showers and perhaps even some thunderstorms. \nHowever, it should be noted that guidance has been varying on \nthe exact arrival time of these troughs and their associated \nrain thus making the specifics of this part of the forecast \nlower confidence. Despite the uncertainty, a period of wet \nweather looks likely mid to late week. \n\nBehind these troughs, guidance is even more uncertain as to \nwhether or not the upper level pattern will become more zonal \n(east-west oriented) or remain amplified heading into next \nweekend. If the zonal pattern becomes reality then we could be \nlooking at another period of tranquil weather for next weekend, \nbut if the amplified pattern materializes then things could \nremain active. With the large amount of uncertainty confidence \nbeyond Thursday is obviously very low, but something we will be \nkeeping an eye on. Regardless, seasonable temperatures are \nforecast to remain through at least next week.\n\nYack",
